## Session 2026-08-22 (PR #30 merged: coverage gap + mbco CI fix)
- Used rforge impact analysis to scope test-coverage work by real ecosystem
  usage: only `missingmed` depends on RMediation (via `mbco()` and `tidy()`
  directly), so zero-coverage files were prioritized by that, not by file size.
- Added 8 new test files (mbco_parametric/.mbco_semi, tidy() dispatch, pMC/qMC,
  utils_validation, utils_stats). Coverage: 55.45% -> 71.70%. Tests: 345 -> 424,
  0 fail.
- PR #30 CI went red (5/5 checks) on `mbco_parametric`'s bootstrap test:
  `Error: Cannot bootstrap null model 'H0'` on macOS/Windows/ubuntu-release +
  the coverage job. Root cause (found by reading further up the CI log than
  the try-error's generic message surfaces): `mxCompare(..., boot = TRUE)`
  calls `OpenMx::mxGenerateData()`, which needs `mvtnorm` — an OpenMx
  `Suggests`, not a hard dependency, so CI's dependency installer (which
  resolves RMediation's own DESCRIPTION) never pulled it in. It worked
  locally only because `mvtnorm` was already present as a transitive
  dependency of something else installed. Fix: added `mvtnorm` to
  RMediation's own `Suggests` + `skip_if_not_installed("mvtnorm")` on the
  affected test.
  - A first fix attempt (pin `OpenMx` to 1 thread, grow fixture `n` to 500)
    was wrong on both counts: it didn't touch the actual bug, and its
    unguarded top-level `OpenMx::mxOption()` call broke `ubuntu-latest
    (devel)` (which doesn't have OpenMx installed) — a regression on a
    previously-passing platform. Reverted.
  - Separately (a genuine improvement, kept even though it wasn't the actual
    CI cause): `H0`/`H1` are now built from scratch instead of one being
    derived by mutating the other's already-run `MxModel` object, which
    removes OpenMx's "modified since it was run" warning.
- All 6 checks green (5 R-CMD-check platforms + test-coverage + codecov),
  424/424 tests, `R CMD check --as-cran` 0/0/0 in the worktree. Squash-merged
  to `dev` (commit 9874187). Worktree + feature branch cleaned up.
- Re-verified on `dev` post-merge: `R CMD check --as-cran` 0 errors/0 warnings
  (1 local-only NOTE — HTML Tidy version, not CRAN-relevant), 424/424 tests,
  coverage 71.70% confirmed via `covr::package_coverage()`.
- No `R/` source files changed — test-only + one new `Suggests` entry. CRAN
  hold from 2026-08-17 is untouched by this session; nothing here requires or
  justifies lifting it.

## Session 2026-08-22 (cross-repo cross-check: manuscript's own hcubature/gauss investigation)
- The companion "product of three" manuscript (same author, private repo) has its own reference
  implementation of this same integrator and ran a far more rigorous investigation of the
  hcubature failure mode than `SPEC-27-prod3-integrator.md` did — Wolfram-arbitrated, 1000
  random correlation matrices, Fisher-exact condition-number association test, blast-radius
  audit — predating SPEC-27 (2026-08-01/02 vs SPEC-27's 2026-08-16) and not consulted while
  writing it. A session working from the manuscript side drafted, then superseded, a plan to
  flip the manuscript's own default to match RMediation before checking this.
- Cross-referenced into `SPEC-27-prod3-integrator.md` (§"Cross-check against the companion
  manuscript"): two refinements, neither overturning the v1.7.0 design decision itself. (1) κ is
  associated with the defect but not a magnitude predictor across matrices generally — this
  spec's own single-ρ-family table (strictly increasing error, κ=4→24139) doesn't generalize;
  the manuscript's sweep found a confirmed-defective matrix at κ=9106 with *smaller* error
  (6.28e-5) than this spec's κ=2411 row (0.196). (2) **`gauss` is not universally safe even on
  well-conditioned input** — a fixed-node GL rule was itself wrong (1.24e-5) at a well-conditioned
  point (κ=4.9) where `hcubature` was accurate. Open question, not yet checked: whether
  RMediation's auto-escalating 256↔512 self-consistency gate (the actual shipped mechanism, not
  a fixed node count) would catch that specific failure. Worth a dedicated follow-up before
  treating the escalation gate as a universal safety net — **relevant to the still-open CRAN
  hold above**, since it bears on the `method="gauss"` default's own safety margin, not just on
  the manuscript's choice. **Tracked as issue #31** (opened 2026-08-22, author approved).
- **Downstream dependency on the CRAN hold (author decision D0a, 2026-08-22 14:01 MDT):** the
  manuscript's cross-reference sentence (approved wording in the manuscript repo's
  `docs/specs/SPEC-rmediation-default-crossref-2026-08-22.md`, destined for its §5.4) is
  **deliberately not inserted** until this package's `method="gauss"` default is either shipped to
  CRAN or reverted. Whichever way the hold resolves, tell the manuscript side: ship → paste the
  approved text; revert → close that spec with no insertion.
- **D3a applied — `NEWS.md` 1.7.0 entry gained one bullet** stating the measurement conditions
  behind the "24% / 92%" figures (q = 0.5, μ = (0.2, 0.1, 0), unit variances, ρ_XZ = ρ_YZ = 0.5,
  ρ_XY ∈ {0.5, 0.99, 0.999, 0.9999}, reference = GL-1024 agreeing with GL-512 to ≤3e-7 and within
  3 SE of 1e7-draw MC) and that the error is not monotone in the condition number across
  arbitrary matrices. ⚠️ The first draft of this bullet said "μ = 0, Z independent" — wrong; SPEC-27
  never states its Σ/μ, so it was re-derived by reproducing SPEC-27's own table values
  (gauss 0.806715023 vs 0.80671500; hcubature 0.610967066 vs 0.6109671) from the regression
  test's `mk()`/`mu` in `tests/testthat/test-product-normal3.R:50-51`. Purpose: a referee
  reading the package changelog beside the manuscript's 9.2e-4 (q = 0, 1000 random matrices,
  exact anchor) can reconcile the two without either document importing the other's evidence.
  Docs-only; the tag `v1.7.0` predates this bullet, so the GitHub pre-release notes do not carry
  it — the CRAN tarball built from `dev` will.
- No code changed this session; `R/prod3_core.R` and the CRAN hold are both untouched. This is a
  documentation cross-reference only. Manuscript-side detail: `docs/REPORT-phase1-prevalence-2026-08-02.md`,
  `docs/REPORT-phase4-blast-radius-2026-08-02.md` Stage D (manuscript repo, private).

